<article class="env-lemma" id="09SK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09SK">Lemma <span data-tag="09SK">13.36.4</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{D}$ be a triangulated category. Let $E, K$ be objects of $\mathcal{D}$. The following are equivalent </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (E, K[i]) = 0$ for all $i \in \mathbf{Z}$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (E', K) = 0$ for all $E' \in \langle E \rangle $. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication (2) $\Rightarrow $ (1) is immediate. Conversely, assume (1). Then $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (X, K) = 0$ for all $X$ in $\langle E \rangle _1$. Arguing by induction on $n$ and using Lemma <a href="/tag/0149" data-tag="0149">13.4.2</a> we see that $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (X, K) = 0$ for all $X$ in $\langle E \rangle _ n$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>